  <!-- § 08 the e-rupee. Inputs: payments-erupee (every line). Prose: evergreen + governed As-of. -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">08</span><h2>The digital rupee</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">e₹ in circulation, end-March</span></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The e-rupee (e₹) is money issued by the RBI itself in digital form, like a banknote that lives in a phone wallet. It is not UPI: a UPI payment moves money between two bank accounts, while an e-rupee is the RBI's own liability, held in a wallet a bank provides. The RBI began pilots for banks and large institutions (wholesale) in November 2022 and for the public (retail) in December 2022.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-erupee" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The e-rupee is a rounding error beside cash</h3>
            <div class="sub">e₹ in circulation on 31 March, ₹ crore, retail and wholesale, 2023 to {e.dates.at(-1).slice(0, 4)}.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-erupee">–</div><div class="c">e₹ in circulation, {erLast}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend">
            <span class="chip"><i class="sq" style="background:#2E4BD8;width:10px;height:10px"></i>Retail</span>
            <span class="chip"><i class="sq" style="background:#C9CED6;width:10px;height:10px"></i>Wholesale</span>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-erupee" style="position:relative"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, Handbook of Statistics on the Indian Economy, e₹ in Circulation (DBIE report 1404). The RBI Innovation Hub's copy of the same table matches it cell for cell.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Each bar is the e-rupee outstanding on one 31 March. The RBI's table counts e-rupee held, not how often it is spent, so we do not know how many people use it or for what. Retail e-rupee is issued in tokens of fixed denominations, like notes.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {erLast}</span>
            <p>e₹ in circulation was <b id="ex-er-now">–</b> in {erLast}, against <b id="ex-er-prev">–</b> a year earlier: <b id="ex-er-share">–</b> of the currency the public held. The RBI's table for {erLast} puts <b id="ex-er-one">–</b> of the retail e-rupee in ₹1 tokens and <b id="ex-er-500">–</b> in ₹500 tokens, where a year earlier ₹500 tokens held <b id="ex-er-500p">–</b>. The table does not say why, and we do not know.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 09 misreadings -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">09</span><h2>How to misread these numbers</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li><span class="no">1</span><div><h3>"UPI has replaced cash."</h3><p>It has replaced a great many cash payments, but the cash the public holds has kept growing. People draw money from ATMs less often and in larger amounts. Both things are true at once, and the RBI's tables cannot say how many payments are still made in notes.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">2</span><div><h3>"Debit card payments collapsed in 2019."</h3><p>Part of the fall is a change in the table. Until October 2019 a debit card's figures included cash drawn at ATMs; from November 2019 those are a separate line. Compare the two layouts only after reading what each line counts.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">3</span><div><h3>"India's digital payments are worth many times its GDP."</h3><p>They are, and it means little. Payment values count every transfer, including banks and companies moving the same money back and forth through RTGS. GDP counts what is produced once. The two are different kinds of number.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">4</span><div><h3>"The average UPI payment is <span id="mr-avg">–</span>, so that is what a UPI payment looks like."</h3><p>An average is pulled up by a few large transfers, so an ordinary UPI payment may be much smaller. The RBI does not publish the typical payment, so we cannot say how small.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">5</span><div><h3>"Payments grew fast, so spending grew as fast."</h3><p>The number of payments grows when the same spending is split into more, smaller payments, as it is when tea and vegetables move to UPI. Growth in payment counts is a sign of a habit changing, not a measure of consumption.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">6</span><div><h3>"The e-rupee is UPI."</h3><p>UPI moves money between bank accounts. The e-rupee is a different form of money, issued by the RBI and held in a separate wallet. The two can work together, but the RBI counts them in separate tables.</p></div></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

